        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "One theory of the success of deep learning models for supervised learning revolves around their ability to learn mappings from the input space to a lower dimensional abstract representation space which are best predictive of the corresponding labels (Tishby & Zaslavsky, 2015). However, additional constraints are necessary for these representations to be robust to noise and adversarial examples, transferable to different domain settings and distribution shifts and interpretable for explainable predictions. As a promising solution to this, the networks can be constrained to encode each distinctive property of the data distribution into disjoint subspaces of the latent representation and then use these representations for downstream tasks. This process of encoding the distinctive properties, or factors of variations, into disjoint subspaces is known as disentanglement.\nFrom a generative perspective, higher dimensional data is assumed to be a non-linear mapping of these factors of variation, where each factor assumes different values to generate specific examples in the higher dimensional data manifold. The generative definition of disentanglement, first proposed in Bengio (2013), proposes to discover these different factors from the data, encoding each factor in a separate subspace or dimension of the learned latent representation. Locatello et al. (2019) intuitively characterizes representations which encode the factors, as disentangled, if a change in a factor produces a change only in the corresponding dimensions of the representation that encode the factor. Alternatively, a change in one dimension of the representation changes only the corresponding factor of variation in the data while being invariant to changes in other factors. These disentangled representations are not only interpretable and give valuable insights into the data distribution but are also more robust for various downstream tasks (Bengio, 2013; Schoelkopf et al., 2012) which might depend only on a subset of factors (Suter et al., 2019).\nThe problem of unsupervised disentanglement is particularly difficult as it proposes to discover the factors of variations without knowing the kind of the factors of variation nor even the number of factors. This makes the task much more challenging, and necessitates inductive biases in the model\ndesign or the data for a one to one mapping between the representations and the factors of variations (Locatello et al., 2019).\nIt has also been argued recently in Zhang et al. (2021); Wilson & Izmailov (2020) that a major fundamental property of deep neural networks is the inductive bias, or the structural knowledge we impart into the network before training. Networks with inductive biases that more closely reflect the data are better able to capture and learn the true underlying distribution.\nWhile it has been claimed, in recent work, that unsupervised disentanglement is possible, if suitable inductive biases are introduced into the network and learning process (Higgins et al., 2018; Locatello et al., 2019), so far finding a good bias is an open question. Here, we posit the most critical of these is that the model can be enforced to map changes in the latent space to changes in the data space that resemble changes in the factors of variations. For truly disentangled representations, the changes in a disentangled factor of representation should be unique and consistent with the changes in the representation space. In addition, this mapping from latents to data space should be locally isometric: The recent work of Horan et al. (2021) demonstrated empirically that the concept of local isometry was a good inductive bias for unsupervised disentanglement, and it can aid generative models in discovering a \u201cnatural\u201d decomposition of data into factors of variation. We take this observation as a starting point, and propose building a novel deep generative neural network model for unsupervised disentanglement that is trained end-to-end with this constraint in mind. If we want to learn these factors in an unsupervised manner the model must have some component that can generate these kinds of changes in the data, as well as a component that is able to discern the changes.\nTo address both of these constraints, the local isometry and the unique and consistent changes, we develop a novel deep generative model, ProtoVAE, consisting of a Prototypical Network and Variational Autoencoder (VAE), which affords both the mode-covering properties of a VAE and the interpretability of GANs. Prototypical Networks Snell et al. (2017) learn a metric space where examples of the same class cluster near the class prototypes and the prototypes of different classes are embedded into separate regions in metric space. To learn these representations in an unsupervised way, we train the prototypical network using self-supervision as described below to constrain the VAE to encode the information corresponding to different factors in separate subspaces. This is achieved via transforming the encoded latent space of the data in different ways and subsequently decoded, which are paired with the original data and passed into the prototypical network. The prototypical network constrains this mapping from the representation space to the data space to be consistent with the desired properties discussed above. These transformations in the latent representation space are done via the approach of interventions (Sec 2.3). We call the changes in the data space induced by interventions in the latent space actions. The prototypical network\u2019s main role is to detect and isolate unique changes in the discovered generative factors resulting from intervention. This process allows us to perform disentanglement without knowledge of the underlying data distribution.\nOur core contributions are such:\n\u2022 Design a self-supervised data generation mechanism using a VAE that creates new samples via a process of intervention to train a metric learning prototypical network.\n\u2022 Design and implement a novel model, ProtoVAE, which combines a VAE and prototypical network to perform disentanglement without any prior knowledge of the underlying data.\n\u2022 Empirically evaluate ProtoVAE on standard benchmark DSprites, 3DShapes, MPI3D, and CelebA datasets, showing state of the art results."
